window.__hvBdRegister("enquiry-into-plants", {"key":"enquiry-into-plants","sections":[{"i":1,"j":15,"loc":"1.1.1–1.1.14","title":"What counts as a part of a plant","summary":"Book I opens by naming what a study of plants must cover: their parts, qualities, ways of originating and life-history. The author finds 'parts' hardest to define, since flowers, leaves and fruit last only a year, and he concludes that plants should not be forced into exact correspondence with animals. He sets out how parts differ (presence or absence, likeness, arrangement) and lists the main 'members': root, stem, branch and twig. Because plants are so various, with no organ common to all as mouth and stomach are to animals, trees are taken as the standard for describing the rest.","ideas":["Annual organs such as flower and fruit complicate the notion of a permanent part","Analogy with animals is useful but must not be pressed where it fails","Parts differ by possession, by likeness or size, and by position","Root, stem, branch and twig are the main members; trees serve as the standard"],"terms":[{"t":"meros ('part')","d":"A permanent component belonging to the plant's nature; the author debates whether annual organs qualify."},{"t":"'composed of like parts'","d":"Said of tissues whose portions share the whole's name, unlike members such as the trunk."}]},{"i":16,"j":34,"loc":"1.2.1–1.4.3","title":"Tissues, the four classes, and habitat","summary":"The author lists the materials of plants: bark, wood and core, and beneath them sap, fibre, veins and flesh, with moisture and warmth as essential. He defines tree, shrub, under-shrub and herb, but warns that cultivation blurs these classes, as when mallow grows tree-like. Distinctions of wild and cultivated, fruitful and barren, evergreen and deciduous are treated as typical rather than strict; he cites Hippon's view that any plant becomes wild or cultivated by attention. The section ends with the division between aquatic and land plants, noting that plants are bound to the ground and situation matters.","ideas":["Plant tissues are named by analogy with animal muscle, veins, flesh and marrow","Tree, shrub, under-shrub and herb are defined, but cultivation can shift a plant between them","Definitions should be typical, not precise, since nature follows no hard and fast law","Habitat, wet or dry, marks a natural division like that among animals"],"terms":[{"t":"core (heart, marrow)","d":"The middle of the wood, third in order from the bark, corresponding to marrow in bones."},{"t":"under-shrub","d":"A plant rising from the root with many stems and many branches, such as savory-rue."}]},{"i":35,"j":52,"loc":"1.5.1–1.7.4","title":"Differences in stem, bark, core and roots","summary":"The author surveys how stems vary in height, straightness and number, and how bark differs in thickness, smoothness, layering and fleshiness, tracing such traits to each plant's essential character. Cores are fleshy, woody or membranous and differ in colour and visibility. Roots differ in number, depth, stoutness and texture; bulbs and tubers such as squill and cuckoo-pint raise the question whether they are true roots, and he insists that definition must rest on function, not position. He adds that roots grow first, go no deeper than the sun's warmth reaches, and notes the banyan and the herb near Opus that roots from its leaves.","ideas":["Properties of wood, such as splitting or bending, follow from grain and sap","Bulbous plants seem to have two kinds of root, one feeding the other","A part is a root by its function, not by being underground","The Lyceum plane sent roots thirty-three cubits when it had room"],"terms":[{"t":"'head'","d":"The solid bulb of plants like squill and onion, likened to an embryo or fruit formed underground."}]},{"i":53,"j":64,"loc":"1.8.1–1.9.7","title":"Knots, growth habit and the fall of leaves","summary":"Trees differ in the number and arrangement of knots, which vary with nature and situation: mountain, wild, 'male' and widely spaced trees have more. Knots may be regular, opposite or random, blind or productive, and cutting a stem creates a new one. Growth in height, branching and stoutness depends much on spacing, pruning and congenial position. The author then lists evergreen trees, shrubs and herbs, notes local exceptions such as the Gortyna plane under which Zeus lay with Europa, and observes that leaf-fall does not track the time of leafing.","ideas":["Shade and close planting give tall, knotless trees; open growth gives knotty, crooked ones","Pruning and position shape a tree's form as much as its kind","Some trees keep their leaves only in particular places, as in Egypt or Crete","Evergreens shed and renew leaves gradually, chiefly around the summer solstice"],"terms":[{"t":"blind knot","d":"A knot from which no growth comes, by nature or through mutilation."}]},{"i":65,"j":80,"loc":"1.10.1–1.12.4","title":"Leaves, seeds, tastes and smells","summary":"The author describes leaves that change form with age, leaves that turn over after the solstice, and the differences between upper and under surfaces, rejecting the view that nourishment passes from the lower side to the upper. He catalogues leaf shapes, textures, divisions, spines and modes of attachment, then the materials of leaves, flowers and fruits. Seeds are said to need moisture and warmth like eggs, and are classed as enclosed in pods, husks, vessels or naked, single or massed. He closes with the tastes and scents of fruits and saps, holding that each plant's flavour reflects its own temperament.","ideas":["Leaves of abele, ivy and castor-oil plant change shape as they age","Seeds may be in a pod, a husk, a vessel, or naked; no tree has naked seeds","Saps and flavours correspond to each plant's special character","The lime is odd: animals eat its sweet leaves but not its fruit"],"terms":[{"t":"'matter' and 'form'","d":"The author's terms for the general sap of a plant and the distinct flavour it matures into in the fruit."}]},{"i":81,"j":92,"loc":"1.13.1–1.14.5","title":"Flowers, bearing fruit, and whole-plant differences","summary":"Flowers are described as downy or 'leafy', single or two-coloured, and placed above, around or on top of the fruit; some, as on cucumbers, are sterile. The author reports that the 'male' date-palm flowers while the 'female' fruits without a flower. Trees bear on new shoots, on last year's wood or both, at the top, the sides or on the stem itself, as the sycamore does. Book I ends by naming the differences that affect a plant's whole being, chief among them water versus land, and by contrasting cultivated kinds, which are named and studied, with wild kinds, known mostly as male or female.","ideas":["The olive's fallen flower shows a hole when it has set fruit","Sterile flowers may grow alongside fertile ones, as in the citron","Fig and vine bear on new shoots; olive, apple and almond on last year's wood","Cultivated kinds are better named because people use and observe them"],"terms":[{"t":"'twofold' flower","d":"A flower with another flower inside it, as in the rose, lily and violet."}]},{"i":93,"j":108,"loc":"2.1.1–2.2.11","title":"Ways plants originate and degenerate","summary":"Book II lists the ways trees arise: spontaneously, from seed, root, a piece torn off, branch, trunk or cut pieces of wood, and notes which trees use which; the olive grows in the most ways. Slips and suckers reproduce the parent faithfully, but trees raised from seed mostly degenerate, as fig, vine, olive, pear and apple do. Position and climate can also change plants, so that some become barren when moved, as the date-palm in Hellas. The author dismisses reports that wheat turns into barley as fabulous and treats cultivation and neglect as moving a plant toward a better or worse form.","ideas":["Growth from seed or root is most natural; other methods depend on human choice","Seedlings of cultivated trees usually lose the parent's quality","Locality matters more than cultivation for fruitfulness","Cultivation and neglect make wild and cultivated forms, not new kinds"],"terms":[{"t":"piece torn off","d":"A slip pulled from the tree with part of the root or stock, a quick and vigorous way to propagate."}]},{"i":109,"j":122,"loc":"2.3.1–2.5.8","title":"Portents, induced changes and how to plant","summary":"The author reports spontaneous changes of fruit or whole trees that soothsayers call portents, while noting they ignore ordinary changes of the same kind; he judges change in the fruit more natural than change in the whole tree. In annual plants changes are produced on purpose, as when bruised one-seeded wheat becomes wheat, and soaking or timing of sowing improves pulses. He compares these with seasonal changes in animals and insect metamorphosis, finding no true analogy. He then gives advice on planting: choice of stock, depth of holes, how to set slips and cuttings, spacing, and above all suiting each tree to its soil.","ideas":["A sour pomegranate turning sweet is counted a worse portent than the reverse","Treatments of seed can make pulses easier to cook or larger","Roots may run deeper than the foot and a half some claim","There are said to be as many kinds of vine as kinds of soil"],"terms":[{"t":"teras (portent)","d":"A change in a tree or its fruit taken by soothsayers as contrary to nature."}]},{"i":123,"j":134,"loc":"2.6.1–2.6.12","title":"The date-palm: propagation and kinds","summary":"The date-palm is propagated by setting several seeds together so their shoots join in one stem, or from the cut-off 'head' of the tree. It likes salt soil and irrigation; growers sprinkle salt around it, and opinions differ on whether dung helps. The author describes transplanting and pruning, the practices of Babylon and Syria, and the many kinds of palm: barren and fruitful, 'male' and 'female', the royal palm, dwarf Cypriot palms, forked palms, the doum-palm of Ethiopia and the dwarf-palm, which shares only the name. He ends by noting that some set cuttings of other trees upside down.","ideas":["Several seeds planted together combine into one strong stem","Salt soil is the sign and condition of good palm country","Dates that keep grow only in the Valley of Syria","The dwarf-palm survives topping and shoots from the root, unlike true palms"],"terms":[{"t":"doum-palm","d":"A shrubby Ethiopian palm with several or forked stems and a rounder, pleasanter fruit."}]},{"i":135,"j":146,"loc":"2.7.1–2.8.4","title":"Cultivation, manure, caprification and palm pollination","summary":"The common tasks of tendance are digging, watering, manuring, pruning and clearing dead wood, applied in degrees suited to each tree; Androtion's advice on pruning olive, myrtle and pomegranate is reported. Manures are ranked, with human dung strongest, after Chartodras. Remedies for barren or leafy trees include splitting the stem and inserting a stone, root-pruning, and 'punishing' the almond with pegs. The author then explains caprification, in which gall-insects from wild figs enter cultivated figs and keep them from dropping, and the dusting of 'male' palm flowers over the 'female' fruit.","ideas":["Each tree needs tendance suited to its nature; the cypress dies if overwatered when young","Dust raked over cucumbers and grapes is said to sweeten them","Wild-fig gall-insects keep cultivated figs from shedding fruit","The 'male' palm's bloom shaken over the 'female' makes it hold its fruit"],"terms":[{"t":"caprification","d":"Hanging wild figs among cultivated ones so gall-insects from them make the figs swell and persist."},{"t":"kentrines","d":"A sluggish kind of gall-insect that kills the useful ones entering the figs."}]},{"i":147,"j":159,"loc":"3.1.1–3.2.5","title":"Origins of wild trees and spontaneous growth","summary":"Book III turns to wild trees, which grow from seed or root, perhaps only because no one tries other methods; even elm and willow, thought fruitless, are shown to reproduce, as after the flood at Pheneos. The author reports the theories of Anaxagoras, Diogenes and Kleidemos on spontaneous generation, calling it beyond the senses, and describes observable cases after floods, heavy rain or stirred soil. He then defines wild trees by late fruiting, vigour and abundant but unripened fruit, and corrects Hippon: neglect makes any plant wild, but not every plant improves by attention. Mountains, with their range of positions, bear all kinds.","ideas":["A plane once came up in a brass pot, proof that it grows from seed","Floods and rain carry seeds and stir the soil, producing new growth","Some plants, like silphium, caper and lupin, are wild in essence and refuse cultivation","Great mountains offer every kind of site and so bear every kind of tree"],"terms":[{"t":"spontaneous generation","d":"Growth without seed, which natural philosophers explain from air, water and earth."}]},{"i":160,"j":175,"loc":"3.3.1–3.5.6","title":"Mountain trees, fruiting and seasons of budding","summary":"Taking Macedonia as an example, the author lists trees found only in mountains and those also in the plain, and says plain trees are taller but mountain trees give better timber and fruit, except pear and apple. He lists wild evergreens, discusses disputed fruitfulness of willow, black poplar and elm, and notes that place decides whether persea and date-palm fruit. Budding comes in spring for all, but times of flowering and ripening vary widely and are set out tree by tree. Fir, silver-fir and oak bud three times with intervals, when galls form and timber should be cut, and some trees bear 'winter buds' or catkins.","ideas":["Black poplars bearing fruit are reported at the Idaean cave in Crete","Phoenician cedar and kermes-oak hold fruit about a year","Oak, fir and silver-fir make three separate flushes of growth","The filbert's catkin grows through winter and falls as the nuts form"],"terms":[{"t":"winter bud","d":"A leaf-like or swollen bud formed before or after leafing that lasts through winter, as in sorb and oak."}]},{"i":176,"j":190,"loc":"3.6.1–3.8.6","title":"Growth, roots, oak growths and kinds of oak","summary":"The author classes trees as quick or slow growing and contrasts the regular branching of the silver-fir with growth from side-buds in walnut and filbert. He reports differing Arcadian and Idaean accounts of which trees root deep. The silver-fir dies if topped but forms a hard 'amphauxis' if cut lower. Trees bear extra growths besides fruit, and the oak bears the most: galls, balls, and a honey-like juice that he thinks comes from the air rather than, as Hesiod says, from the tree. The section ends with the kinds of oak as distinguished on Mount Ida and in Macedonia, by acorn, timber and galls.","ideas":["The silver-fir's black, hard 'amphauxis' is used for mixing-bowls","Only hemeris gives a gall fit for tanning","Aeolians say only the sea-bark oak is struck by lightning","Wood of the aspris is poor except for the smith's fire"],"terms":[{"t":"amphauxis","d":"A hard black growth that forms around the stump of a silver-fir topped in its smooth part."},{"t":"hemeris (gall-oak)","d":"The Idaean oak kind whose gall is used for tanning hides."}]},{"i":191,"j":203,"loc":"3.9.1–3.11.2","title":"Firs, pines, beech, yew, lime, maple, ash","summary":"The author distinguishes the Idaean and seaside firs, 'male' and 'female' firs, and the Arcadians' claim that some so-called firs are pines; he describes resin, the valued heart-wood 'aigis', and the fir's pitch-glutted disease. The silver-fir is compared with the fir in leaf, wood and knots, its white centre used for painters' boards and writing-tablets. Shorter descriptions follow of beech, yew, hop-hornbeam (held unlucky in the house), lime with its male and female forms, the maples, and two kinds of ash. He closes with a general rule that mountain trees are harder and better coloured, plain trees larger.","ideas":["Woodmen say 'male' timber is shorter, more twisted and darker","A burnt fir does not shoot again; a burnt pine may, as at Pyrrha in Lesbos","Yew leaves kill beasts of burden but not ruminants","Lime bark is used for writing-cases"],"terms":[{"t":"aigis","d":"The resin-rich heart of the fir, cut from fallen trees once the outer wood has rotted."}]},{"i":204,"j":212,"loc":"3.12.1–3.13.7","title":"Cornel, cedars, medlars, sorb, bird-cherry, elder, willow","summary":"The cornelian cherry's 'male' wood is hard as horn, while the 'female' cornel is softer and useless for javelins. Two 'cedars' are compared, the fragrant prickly cedar and arkeuthos, whose fruit stays on the tree for a year or more. The three kinds of mespile and the sorb are described in leaf, flower, fruit, wood and pests, with the sorb's compound leaf falling whole. The bird-cherry's spirally peeling bark is used for writing-cases; the elder's hollow boughs make walking-sticks and its juice is used in initiations. Black and white willows end the section.","ideas":["Medlar fruit is larger and softer; the other kinds store longer","Sorb fruit is worm-eaten before it ripens yet is very astringent","The bird-cherry's bark grows in a spiral round the trunk","Initiates bathe hands and heads in elder-berry juice"],"terms":[{"t":"arkeuthos (Phoenician cedar)","d":"A cedar-like tree with soft leaves and black, astringent fruit that ripens slowly."}]},{"i":213,"j":232,"loc":"3.14.1–3.17.6","title":"Elm, poplars, filbert, terebinth, oaks, and local trees","summary":"The author describes the elm with its 'wallets' of gum and gnats, the abele, black poplar, aspen and alder, then semyda, bladder-senna, the filbert (wild by nature but open to cultivation), the terebinth, box and krataigos. Kermes-oak may carry four fruits at once, counting mistletoe and oak-mistletoe; the Arcadian smilax and 'cork-oak' (aria) are placed between kermes-oak and oak. Arbutus, andrachne and the wig-tree, whose fruit turns to down, follow. He turns to local trees: the Tyrrhenian cork-oak stripped for bark, the kolutea of the Lipari islands, and trees peculiar to Mount Ida.","ideas":["Corsican box is so abundant that local honey tastes of it","Terebinth grows tall at Damascus but low and twisted on Ida","Cork-oak bark must be fully stripped and renews in about three years","Kolutea pods fatten sheep wonderfully"],"terms":[{"t":"memaikylon","d":"The edible fruit of the arbutus, which takes a year to ripen."}]},{"i":233,"j":242,"loc":"3.18.1–3.18.10","title":"Shrubs: buckthorn, bramble, sumach, ivy, smilax","summary":"Variation within a kind is shown among shrubs: black and white buckthorn and withy, Christ's thorn whose seeds doctors use for coughs, and kinds of bramble including the 'dog's bramble'. Sumach is used by tanners and has a clustered fruit. Ivy receives the longest account: white, black and helix, and the dispute whether the helix turns into true ivy with age; ivy roots from its shoots and starves the tree it climbs. The smilax's leaf and fruit are described, and Book III ends with the foul-smelling spindle-tree of Lesbos, deadly to sheep and goats.","ideas":["Some say helix is a distinct kind; others that it becomes ivy as it ages","Ivy's aerial roots draw moisture from its host","Birds eat sweet ivy berries but avoid bitter ones","The spindle-tree's flower smells like shed blood"],"terms":[{"t":"helix","d":"A barren kind of ivy with small angular leaves, said by some to develop into true ivy."}]},{"i":243,"j":260,"loc":"4.1.1–4.2.12","title":"Proper positions and the trees of Egypt","summary":"Book IV holds that every tree grows best in its own position: some love wet, some sun, some shade, and north aspects give closer wood. Silver-firs in a sunless hollow near Krane grow tall but coarse, while sun-grown masts are short and strong; trees set close make the best oar-spars. The author then describes trees peculiar to Egypt: the sycamore that fruits on its stem and must be scraped to ripen, the Cretan 'Cyprian fig', the carob, persea, balanos, doum-palm and acacia. He adds olives of the Thebaid, the Egyptian plum, and a bush near Memphis whose leaves collapse when touched and later revive.","ideas":["Sycamore wood is seasoned by sinking it in deep water","The persea bears continuously, new fruit overtaking old","Black acacia is used for ship's ribs; its pods tan hides","A Memphis bush's leaves collapse when touched and then recover"],"terms":[{"t":"persea","d":"A large evergreen Egyptian tree with sweet, digestible fruit and dark wood used for images and furniture."}]},{"i":261,"j":279,"loc":"4.3.1–4.4.13","title":"Trees of Libya, Persia and India","summary":"Libya bears the lotos, Christ's thorn, date-palms and silphium; the lotos fed the army of Ophelias on its march against Carthage, and reports of its form and wine are compared. In the rainless region date-palms live on ground-water and dew, and the author doubts whether its animals truly go without drink. In Asia, ivy would not grow in Harpalus' Babylonian gardens, but the Median citron, used against moths and poison, is described with its cultivation. India has the banyan, which drops roots from its branches into a tent-like ring, ebony, fruits Alexander forbade, cotton-trees and rice; Aria and Gedrosia have poisonous plants, and the aromatics of the east are named.","ideas":["Ophelias' army lived for days on lotos fruit alone","Ivy grows on Mount Meros in India, whence Dionysus came","The banyan's shade is said to reach two furlongs","Some Gedrosian plants kill beasts or blind men with their juice"],"terms":[{"t":"lotos","d":"A Libyan tree with sweet bean-sized fruit, eaten by the Lotus-eaters and made into wine."}]},{"i":280,"j":300,"loc":"4.5.1–4.7.3","title":"Northern trees, ship timber and plants of the sea","summary":"The north has only familiar cold-loving trees and medicinal plants, while fragrant plants belong to hot southern places; bay and myrtle fail in cold Pontus despite efforts to grow them for religious use. The author notes how mountains differ in what they bear, that shipbuilding timber comes from few regions, and that the plane is scarce in Italy, even those planted by Dionysius at Rhegium. He then describes sea plants of 'our sea': seaweeds, oyster-green, a Cretan dye-weed, and the 'sea-oak', 'sea-fir', 'sea-vine', 'sea-fig' and 'sea-palm'. Outer seas have the 'sea-leek' and stone-like growths, and the Red Sea has a sea 'bay' and 'olive' whose gum stanches blood.","ideas":["Medicinal roots and juices come from cold regions; aromatics from hot ones","Ship timber comes from Macedonia, parts of Thrace and Italy, and a few Asian districts","The Cretan dye-weed gives a colour finer than purple while fresh","Some outer-sea growths look like stone above water but like plants in leaf"],"terms":[{"t":"'sea-leek' (riband-weed)","d":"A large annual seaweed of the ocean near the pillars of Heracles, carried into the inner sea."}]},{"i":301,"j":317,"loc":"4.7.4–4.8.11","title":"Plants of the outer sea and Egypt","summary":"Theophrastus reports what members of Alexander's expedition returning from India said about plants of the outer sea: sea-growths that turn salt-like in the sun, stone rushes, mangrove-like trees on tidal islands and in Carmania, and the cotton-plant and a leaf that 'sleeps' at night on Tylos. He then turns to plants of rivers, marshes and lakes, dividing them into trees, herbaceous plants and clump-growing plants. Egyptian water plants are described in detail: the papyrus and its many uses, the sari, the 'Egyptian bean' with its honeycomb-like head, and the lotos whose flowers close at sunset. The section ends with malinathalle and fodder plants of Egypt.","ideas":["Trees that grow in the tidal sea seem to draw nourishment from salt water","Water plants fall into trees, herbaceous plants and clump-growing plants","Papyrus serves for fuel, boats, sails, ropes, writing-rolls and food","The lotos closes its flowers at sunset and opens them at sunrise"],"terms":[{"t":"papyrus","d":"The Egyptian marsh plant whose three-cornered stalks and roots supply wood, cordage, writing material and food."},{"t":"lotos (Nile water-lily)","d":"A plant of flooded Egyptian plains whose seed-heads are dried and made into loaves and whose root, korsion, is eaten."}]},{"i":318,"j":328,"loc":"4.9.1–4.10.9","title":"Water-chestnut and the plants of Lake Orchomenos","summary":"Every river has its own plants; the water-chestnut grows only in marshy rivers of moderate depth such as the Strymon, and Theophrastus describes its floating leaves, hair-like growths and hard black fruit, leaving its root and lifespan for further enquiry. He then lists the plants of the lake near Orchomenos and describes the goat-willow, the water-lily and the fruits of sedge and phleos. Galingale and dog's-tooth grass are noted as hardest to kill because of the way their roots spread. He distinguishes plants found both on land and in water from those found only in water, and ends with the edible parts of the lake plants.","ideas":["Each river, like each region of land, bears peculiar plants","Plants that root as strongly downward as upward are hard to destroy","Some lake plants grow on floating islands as well as on the bed","Several points are left as matters for further enquiry"],"terms":[{"t":"water-chestnut","d":"A plant of marshy rivers whose fruit hangs underwater beneath floating leaves."},{"t":"floating islands","d":"Masses of vegetation on lakes such as that of Orchomenos, on which goat-willow, galingale and sedge grow."}]},{"i":329,"j":343,"loc":"4.11.1–4.12.4","title":"Reeds, the pipe-reed and rushes","summary":"Theophrastus distinguishes the reed used for pipes from other reeds and corrects the belief that it grows only once in nine years: it grows whenever the lake stays full for two years, as before the battle of Chaeronea. He records how the cutting season changed after Antigenidas introduced a more elaborate style of playing, and describes in detail how reed-tongues for mouthpieces are made from the internodes. The best reed grows in deep holes in the marsh where the Kephisos deposits rich soil. He then surveys other reeds, including the Cretan archer's reed and the Indian bamboo, three kinds of rush, and closes his account of water plants with the floating islands of Orchomenos and Egypt.","ideas":["Taking an accidental regularity for a natural law gave rise to the nine-year myth","Changes in musical style changed the season for cutting pipe-reeds","Which part of the reed is used determines a reed-tongue's quality","Barren and fruiting rushes were seen growing from one stock"],"terms":[{"t":"reed-tongue","d":"The vibrating piece of a pipe's mouthpiece, cut from a section of reed between two knots."},{"t":"Indian reed (bamboo)","d":"A large reed of the river Akesines with solid and hollow forms, used for javelins."}]},{"i":344,"j":359,"loc":"4.13.1–4.14.13","title":"Length of life and diseases of trees","summary":"Water plants are said to be shorter-lived than land plants, and wild trees generally longer-lived than cultivated ones; mythical examples such as the olive at Athens and the palm in Delos testify to great age. Theophrastus asks whether a tree that shoots again from its stock is the same tree, weighs the trunk against the root as its essential part, and reports a method of renewing vines by cutting their roots on alternate sides. He then reviews diseases: wild trees suffer only from bad weather, while cultivated trees suffer worms, sun-scorch, rot, scab, krados and fruit-shedding, some of which depend on locality. The section ends with damage from frost and from the scorching 'Olympian' wind of Chalcis.","ideas":["Wild forms outlive cultivated forms of the same tree","A tree's identity may be judged by its trunk or by its starting-point","Many diseases may come from blows or wounds to the roots","Some injuries from season and place are not properly diseases"],"terms":[{"t":"krados","d":"A fig disease in which the branches turn black, named from kradoi, a word for branches."},{"t":"sun-scorch","d":"One of the general diseases of cultivated trees, along with worm-damage and rot."}]},{"i":360,"j":368,"loc":"4.15.1–4.16.7","title":"How trees are killed or harmed","summary":"Theophrastus considers what destroys trees. Stripping the bark all round kills almost all of them, though the andrachne and cork-oak lose an outer bark, some trees recover, and the season and width of the stripping matter. Topping kills fir, silver-fir, Aleppo pine and date-palm, while other trees shoot again; splitting the stem kills most trees, but tapping for resin can make some more fruitful. He reports trees that rose again after being hewn or blown down, at Antandros, Philippi and Stageira, and notes death from cutting the roots, from oil, from rival plants and from ivy, ending with the vine's supposed aversion to cabbage.","ideas":["Ringing the bark is a near-universal cause of death","Topping kills a few conifers and the palm but not most trees","Fallen or hewn planes and willows have been seen to revive","Oil and neighbouring plants destroy trees by addition, not removal"],"terms":[{"t":"topping","d":"Cutting off a tree's crown, which kills fir, silver-fir, Aleppo pine and date-palm."}]},{"i":369,"j":384,"loc":"5.1.1–5.2.3","title":"Cutting seasons and the grain of timber","summary":"Book V opens with timber. Wood to be barked is cut when the tree comes into leaf, because the bark then peels easily, but wood is strongest after the fruit ripens; oak is cut last, in early winter. Silver-fir and fir are compared: the fir is fleshy, heavy and resinous, while the silver-fir is fibrous, light, layered like an onion and best for masts and yard-arms. Timber is described as 'four-cleft', 'two-cleft' or 'one-cleft' by its lines of fissure, and wood from north-facing places is better. Theophrastus ranks timber by region, with Macedonian first, and explains knots and 'coiling' as signs of poor nourishment, citing the Megara olive that was found to hold old offerings.","ideas":["The season for barking timber differs from the season for strongest wood","Silver-fir and fir are opposites in flesh, fibre and resin","Lines of fissure decide how timber splits and warps","Vigorous growth can bury knots and even objects set in the wood"],"terms":[{"t":"four-cleft","d":"Timber with two distinct lines of fissure on each side of the heart-wood, the closest-grained and best for carpentry."},{"t":"coiling","d":"Abnormally close twisting of the rings, caused by winter or poor nourishment, and harder to work than knots."}]},{"i":385,"j":403,"loc":"5.3.1–5.5.4","title":"Density, hardness, decay and the core of wood","summary":"Theophrastus compares woods by closeness, weight and hardness: box and ebony are so dense that they sink, while lime is soft and is said to blunt tools by its heat. He notes woods that are tough enough for shields, elm that does not warp and is used for door-hinges, palm-wood for images, and the long-lasting thyon of Cyrene. Cypress, cedar, olive and others resist decay; oak lasts in fresh water and beech when soaked, while the teredon in the sea and skolex and thrips on land bore through timber. Carpenters hold that every wood has a living core that 'draws' and warps the wood unless removed or properly sawn, and timber is described as cleft, hewn or round.","ideas":["Weight follows closeness, moisture and hardness of texture","Resistance to decay depends on the wood and on its exposure","Bitter or hard woods escape boring creatures","The core stays alive and warps the wood when it 'draws'"],"terms":[{"t":"teredon","d":"A small sea creature with a large head and teeth that bores into timber, and whose damage cannot be repaired."},{"t":"core (of wood)","d":"The central part that carpenters say stays alive and 'draws', so they remove it from doors and fine work."}]},{"i":404,"j":417,"loc":"5.6.1–5.8.3","title":"Strength of woods and their uses","summary":"Silver-fir and fir bear weight well and give warning before failing, palm-wood bends upward, and fir takes glue best. Theophrastus discusses which woods saw, bore, plane and bend easily, and why green and dry wood each cause difficulties. He then lists uses: silver-fir for triremes, fir for merchant ships, cedar in Syria and Phoenicia, oak for keels, and many woods for houses, furniture, waggons, looms, walking-sticks and tools. The section ends with regional excellence, including the great trees of Corsica that broke a Roman fleet's masts and the wooded country of the Latins and Circe's promontory.","ideas":["Strength under load differs from strength when upright","Moisture helps sawing but hinders boring and gluing","Each wood has its regular use in ships, houses and tools","Place determines how fine a given timber grows"],"terms":[{"t":"trireme","d":"A warship, built mainly of silver-fir for lightness, with an oak keel for hauling."}]},{"i":418,"j":424,"loc":"5.9.1–5.9.7","title":"Woods for charcoal, fuel and fire-sticks","summary":"Theophrastus considers wood in relation to fire. The best charcoal comes from close woods such as holm-oak and arbutus taken from trees in their prime, though smiths prefer fir charcoal because it blows up into a flame, and different mines use different charcoals. He describes how the charcoal-heap is built, which damp woods give bad smoke, and why fig smoke is pungent. Fire-sticks are best made with the stationary piece of ivy or traveller's joy and the drill of bay, since the two parts should differ in nature. He ends with woods that exude moisture, so that statues are said to 'sweat', and olive-wood that sprouts even when worked.","ideas":["Close, sappy wood makes the strongest charcoal","Open, light or dry wood gives the fiercest flame","The two parts of a fire-stick should be of different woods","Oily woods exude moisture, which explains 'sweating' statues"],"terms":[{"t":"fire-sticks","d":"A stationary piece and a drill rubbed together to kindle fire, ideally ivy or traveller's joy against bay."}]},{"i":425,"j":447,"loc":"6.1.1–6.3.5","title":"Under-shrubs: classes, kinds and silphium","summary":"Book VI turns to under-shrubs, a woody class whose cultivated members are mostly coronary plants. The chief division is between spinous and spineless kinds: some plants are wholly spines, some have spinous leaves, and some have leaves as well as spines. Theophrastus describes paired forms of rock-rose, kneoron, marjoram, thyme, sage, horehound and konyza, and notes that thyme will not grow where no sea-breeze reaches. He then describes ferula and the ferula-like plants, and at length the silphium of Libya, its juices, the regulated cutting of its root, its avoidance of cultivated land and the conflicting reports about it, before distinguishing magydaris.","ideas":["Wild kinds of under-shrub far outnumber cultivated ones","Spinous plants are classed by where their spines grow","Bees' honey yield is judged by the flowering of thyme","Accounts of silphium conflict and are left for enquiry"],"terms":[{"t":"silphium","d":"A ferula-like Libyan plant yielding stalk-juice and root-juice, cut under strict rules and avoiding cultivated ground."},{"t":"narthex (ferula)","d":"A tall jointed plant with an annual stalk, the model for the 'ferula-like' plants."}]},{"i":448,"j":460,"loc":"6.4.1–6.5.4","title":"Spinous plants: thistles, caper and caltrop","summary":"Theophrastus takes the spinous under-shrubs class by class. The wholly spinous class is tiny, essentially asparagus and skorpios, whose flowering and roots he describes. The largest class has spinous leaves and is mostly thistle-like, varying in head, stalks and season; he describes safflower, whose wild form is unusually softer than the cultivated, distaff-thistle with its blood-coloured juice, golden thistle, sow-thistle, ixine and the Sicilian kaktos. Of plants with both leaves and spines he treats caper, caltrop with its spinous fruit, and rest-harrow, a deep-rooting pest of farmers that must be pulled up whole.","ideas":["Spinous plants divide into wholly spinous, spinous-leaved and leaved-and-spinous","Most spinous-leaved plants have a thistle-like head","Wild safflower reverses the usual rule of wild plants being rougher","Rest-harrow spreads by deep roots and is hard to kill"],"terms":[{"t":"kaktos (cardoon)","d":"A Sicilian spinous plant with creeping edible stems that are preserved in brine."}]},{"i":461,"j":479,"loc":"6.6.1–6.8.5","title":"Coronary plants and their flowering seasons","summary":"The cultivated under-shrubs are the coronary plants used for garlands, some valued for the flower and some for the whole fragrant growth. Theophrastus describes the violet, which can flower all year, roses with up to a hundred petals and the fragrant roses of Cyrene, lilies, narcissus and the saffron-crocus, which thrives when trodden. Against those who say thyme and mint bear no seed, he holds that they do, as their wild forms show, and he gives notes on propagating southernwood, marjoram and thyme. He then sets out the order of flowering through spring, summer and autumn, so that no season lacks flowers, and ends with the lifespans of gilliflower and rose and the effect of place on scent.","ideas":["Coronary plants straddle the under-shrubs and the herbaceous plants","Scent and colour depend largely on locality","Wild forms show that thyme and mint reproduce from seed","Flowers follow one another so that no period is flowerless"],"terms":[{"t":"coronary plants","d":"Plants grown for garlands, valued for their flowers or for their fragrant leaves and branches."}]},{"i":480,"j":508,"loc":"7.1.1–7.3.9","title":"Pot-herbs: sowing, germination, roots and seeds","summary":"Book VII begins the herbaceous plants with cultivated pot-herbs. Theophrastus gives the three garden seed-times, named for the season of growth rather than of sowing, and the days each herb takes to germinate, from three for basil and radish to forty or fifty for celery. He considers why germination varies with seed, soil, weather and the age of the seed, and notes that beet seed germinates over several years. He describes propagation from seed, cuttings and roots, regrowth of stems after breaking, and the differences between single, woody, fleshy and short roots. He ends with flowering, the shapes and coverings of seeds, the custom of cursing cummin while sowing, and effects of position on growth.","ideas":["Seed-times are named for the season of use, not of sowing","Germination time differs by herb and by seed age","Herbs are propagated by seed, cuttings or root","Roots are classed as woody or fleshy, long or short"],"terms":[{"t":"secondary crops","d":"Herbs such as beet, lettuce and dill that are sown at all three garden seed-times."}]},{"i":509,"j":524,"loc":"7.4.1–7.5.5","title":"Varieties of pot-herbs and their culture","summary":"Some pot-herbs have one kind and others several, and Theophrastus lists the varieties of radish, turnip, cabbage, beet, lettuce, celery, cucumber, onion and garlic. He reports the claim that thin sowing of turnip gives 'female' plants, describes how the 'divided' onion (shallot) is treated and how the Ascalonian onion differs, and explains how garlic grows from seed over three years. On culture, all herbs except rue love water and dung; he says which manures and waters are best and that transplanting improves most herbs. The section ends with pests such as 'leek-cutters' and spiders, and with which seeds keep longest, none being fit for sowing after four years.","ideas":["Varieties differ in leaf, root, colour and taste","The Ascalonian onion does not divide or multiply from the root","Water and dung benefit all pot-herbs except rue","Pungent seeds keep better than summer-herb seeds"],"terms":[{"t":"leek-cutters","d":"Pests of lettuce and leek that gather in dung heaps, where they can be caught."}]},{"i":525,"j":544,"loc":"7.6.1–7.10.2","title":"Wild herbs and the differences of herbaceous plants","summary":"Wild forms of the pot-herbs are smaller, rougher, less juicy and more pungent; Theophrastus describes wild cabbage, turnip, lettuce with its medicinal juice, and the wild celeries. He extends 'pot-herbs' to uncultivated chicory-like plants such as dandelion and goat's beard and to herbs eaten raw or cooked. He then sets out the differences among herbaceous plants: erect, prostrate or clasping stems, ground or stem leaves, barren or fruiting, and the bright varied flowers of herbs compared with the pale flowers of trees. Roots differ in colour, taste and edibility, and sweet roots may be deadly while bitter ones are beneficial. Unlike trees, herbs grow and flower at different seasons, so that flowering continues throughout the year.","ideas":["Wild herbs are less juicy and therefore more pungent","Herbaceous plants differ in stem, leaf, flower, fruit and root","Taste does not decide whether a root is edible","Herbs flower in succession across all seasons, unlike trees"],"terms":[{"t":"chicory-like plants","d":"Wild herbs such as dandelion, cat's ear and groundsel whose leaves resemble chicory's."}]},{"i":545,"j":570,"loc":"7.10.3–7.15.4","title":"Spiked, chicory-like and bulbous herbaceous plants","summary":"Theophrastus proposes classing herbs by visible features and starts with spiked plants such as rib-grass, fox-brush and plantain. He describes the chicory-like plants, including tough, hard-to-kill chicory and the successive flowering of dandelion and pikris. Bulbous and fleshy-rooted plants follow: edible roots of asphodel, Epimenides' squill, cuckoo-pint and corn-flag, then leaves and stems, the useful asphodel, the long life of squill, and the way squill and narcissus send up the stem before the leaves. He ends with peculiarities: maidenhair, which does not get wet, bedstraw, which keeps its flower inside, flowering tied to the stars and winds, and plants such as lotos and strykhnos whose single name covers very different kinds.","ideas":["Classes should be drawn from visible parts and roots","Squill and narcissus produce stems before leaves","Squill lives on when hung up and preserves stored fruit","One name may cover plants of different properties"],"terms":[{"t":"squill","d":"A bulbous plant, very tenacious of life, whose three flowerings are said to mark the three seed-times."},{"t":"strykhnos","d":"A name covering one edible plant and two others, one sleep-inducing and one causing madness or death."}]},{"i":571,"j":590,"loc":"8.1.1–8.2.11","title":"Cereals and pulses: sowing, sprouting and ripening","summary":"Book VIII treats corn-like plants, pulses and summer crops, all grown from seed. Theophrastus gives the sowing seasons, the setting of the Pleiad and early spring, and explains why robust crops are sown early to withstand winter and some weak ones early to root before it. He gives times of germination, contrasts cereals, which put out root and shoot from opposite ends of the seed, with pulses, which put them out from one point, and describes the jointed stem, the ear and the brief flowering of cereals against the long flowering of the bean. Ripening times vary with soil and climate, as shown by Egypt, Mylae, Melos and Chalkia, where two crops ripen in the time of one.","ideas":["Crops are sown early for robustness or for weakness","Cereals and pulses sprout in different ways","The ear forms inside the stem before it appears","Local soil can greatly speed ripening"],"terms":[{"t":"pulses (legumes)","d":"Plants such as bean, chick-pea and pea that bear pods and flower part by part up the stem."}]},{"i":591,"j":606,"loc":"8.3.1–8.5.4","title":"Differences among cereals, pulses and summer crops","summary":"Theophrastus compares the three classes by leaf, stem, flower and fruit: reed-like leaves and jointed hollow stems in cereals, round or oblong leaves and woody stems in pulses, and the ear, the pod and the 'plume'. Pulses and summer crops are more prolific, while cereal seeds better withstand weather. Wheat and barley are compared in leaf, stem, coats and husk, and the kinds of barley by number of rows and colour of grain. Kinds of wheat are named by region and by ripening time, from forty days to three months, and differ in weight, as with Boeotian wheat, of which athletes eat little. Pulses show fewer recognised kinds, though chick-peas vary, and their pods differ in shape and divisions.","ideas":["The three crop classes differ in every visible part","Barley kinds range from two to six rows of grain","Soil and climate make wheats light or heavy","Pulses are distinguished mainly by their pods"],"terms":[{"t":"three-months wheat","d":"Quick-ripening kinds sown late, generally light and not prolific."}]},{"i":607,"j":626,"loc":"8.6.1–8.8.6","title":"Sowing, rain, soil and crop weeds","summary":"Early sowing is best, and seed density, manuring and ploughing must suit the soil, as Syria and Sicily show. Rain helps crops in leaf but harms cereals in flower, while the bean wants rain when flowering; spring rains make Sicily rich, and dew suffices in rainless lands. Wheat and barley are said to turn into darnel in wet seasons, the chick-pea exhausts the soil while the bean restores it, and grazing young corn in Thessaly and Babylon checks overgrowth. Theophrastus stresses the season, soil, winds and tillage, citing the saying that the year bears and not the field, and warns against mixing foreign and native seed. He ends with weeds and parasites tied to particular crops, such as dodder and broom-rape, and with pulses that will or will not cook.","ideas":["The timing of rain matters more than its amount","Wheat and barley are said to degenerate into darnel","Foreign seed turns native in about three years","Weather and wind can make the same field's pulses uncookable"],"terms":[{"t":"darnel","d":"A weed said to arise from degenerate wheat and barley in rainy districts."},{"t":"'cookable' and 'uncookable'","d":"Terms for pulses that do or do not soften in cooking, a quality caused by soil and climate."}]},{"i":627,"j":646,"loc":"8.9.1–9.1.3","title":"Soil exhaustion, crop diseases, seed-keeping; plant juices","summary":"Wheat exhausts the land most, then barley, while beans seem to manure it; among wheat-like plants zeia and oats are hardest on the soil and one-seeded wheat the lightest. Crop diseases include rust, worst in low windless land and at full moon, wind-bite, scorching and grubs, all depending on climate and place. Seeds differ in how long they keep, and seed a year old sows best, though at Petra in Cappadocia it stays fertile for forty years; lupin must be sown straight from the threshing-floor, and aigilops seed germinates in alternate years. Book IX then opens on plant juices, which differ in taste and colour, and describes the gummy juices that yield resin, frankincense, myrrh, mastic and tragacanth.","ideas":["Crops that root deep and send up many stems exhaust the soil","Low, sheltered ground is most liable to rust","Storage place and dryness at harvest decide how long seed keeps","Fatty gums are fragrant, non-fatty ones scentless"],"terms":[{"t":"rust","d":"A disease common to cereals, worst in barley and in low, windless places."},{"t":"sap (khylos)","d":"The moisture proper to plants, which has distinct qualities of taste and colour in each kind."}]},{"i":647,"j":659,"loc":"9.1.4–9.3.2","title":"Gums, tapping seasons, resin and pitch-making","summary":"Theophrastus finishes his account of plant juices and gums, noting that juice may form naturally, from incisions, or both, and that men only tap plants whose juice is useful. He gives the best seasons for tapping vine, resinous trees, frankincense, myrrh and balsam, and distinguishes the stalk-juice and root-juice of silphium. He then ranks the resins of terebinth, silver-fir, Aleppo pine and fir, reports what the people of Mount Ida and Macedonia say about pitch yields and how trees refill, and describes in detail the Macedonian method of extracting pitch by fire in a great earth-covered pile, with the Syrian method of burning the standing tree.","ideas":["Juice forms naturally or by incision; only useful juices are tapped","Terebinth resin is finest, fir resin most abundant and pitchlike","Pitch quality depends on winter, aspect and shade, not on fruit-bearing","The Macedonian pitch-pile is watched day and night with sacrifice and prayer"],"terms":[{"t":"resinous wood","d":"The pitch-filled wood of the fir, which is cut out or burned to obtain pitch and extends to the roots."},{"t":"stalk-juice and root-juice","d":"The two juices taken from silphium; the root-juice is clearer and better, the stalk-juice needs meal to clot."}]},{"i":660,"j":677,"loc":"9.4.1–9.7.5","title":"Frankincense, myrrh, cinnamon, balsam and perfume plants","summary":"Theophrastus turns to the fragrant plants of the south and east. He reports conflicting accounts of the frankincense and myrrh trees of Arabia, preferring the sailors from the bay of the Heroes, who saw the trees tapped and described the Sabaeans' honest trade at the temple of the sun, where a third of the price goes to the god. He gives accounts of cinnamon and cassia, rejecting as fable the tale of snakes and lots drawn with the sun, then describes balsam of Mecca in its two Syrian parks and the sweet-flag and ginger-grass of Syria. He closes with a list of perfume plants, almost all Asian, of which only the Illyrian iris comes from Europe.","ideas":["Eyewitness reports outweigh contradictory hearsay about frankincense and myrrh","The Sabaean market is left unguarded and run on trust","The cinnamon-and-snakes story is dismissed as fable","The finest aromatics come from Asia and sunny lands; Europe yields only iris"],"terms":[{"t":"balsam of Mecca","d":"A cultivated Syrian tree whose rare, highly fragrant gum is tapped at the Dog-star and sells for twice its weight in silver."},{"t":"komakon","d":"An aromatic from Arabia mixed into the choicest unguents, distinguished from a fruit of the same name."}]},{"i":678,"j":701,"loc":"9.8.1–9.10.6","title":"Medicinal juices, root-cutting lore and hellebore","summary":"Theophrastus begins his account of medicinal plants, explaining that their virtue may lie in root, fruit, juice or leaves, and when and how juices are collected from stalks, roots or the poppy's head. He reports the precautions of druggists and herb-diggers, accepting those that guard against harmful plants but calling far-fetched the rites involving woodpeckers, eagles, circles drawn with a sword and dancing round mandrake. He then lists the uses of mandrake, all-heal, cyclamen, squirting cucumber, germander, thapsia and iskhas, and ends with the white and black hellebore: their contested appearance, effects on animals, best localities such as Oeta and Helicon, and the name of Melampus.","ideas":["Medicinal virtue resides in different parts of different plants","Practical cautions are reasonable; magical rituals of root-cutting are irrelevant additions","Parts of one root may purge upward and others downward","White and black hellebore share little but the name"],"terms":[{"t":"rhizotomoi (root-diggers / herb-diggers)","d":"Gatherers of medicinal roots whose reports Theophrastus records, sometimes approving and sometimes calling them exaggerated."},{"t":"elaterion ('the driver')","d":"A purgative drug made from the extracted juice of the squirting cucumber."}]},{"i":702,"j":727,"loc":"9.11.1–9.14.3","title":"Kinds of all-heal, spurge, poppy and roots","summary":"Theophrastus surveys plants that share one name but form several kinds: the all-heals of Syria, Chaeronea, Asclepios and Herakles; two strykhnos, one inducing sleep and one causing madness in graded doses; several spurges; two libanotis; white and dark chamaeleon; and three wild poppies, with their appearance and remedies. He then classifies roots by taste, smell and colour, from sweet water-lily and Scythian liquorice to roots that derange or kill, and by peculiar shape, as scorpion-plant and polypody. Finally he notes how long various roots keep, with squirting-cucumber juice said to last two hundred years, and that roots and fruit compete for the plant's virtue.","ideas":["One name often covers several distinct plants","The madness-causing strykhnos acts by graded dose, up to death","Roots differ by taste, colour and shape, and some sweet roots are deadly","Pungent roots resist worms; sweet ones become worm-eaten"],"terms":[{"t":"strykhnos","d":"A name for two plants: one soporific, the other (thorn-apple) producing delusion or death depending on dose."},{"t":"tithymallos (spurge)","d":"A group of purgative plants, including sea-spurge, the 'male' and the 'myrtle-like' kinds."}]},{"i":728,"j":746,"loc":"9.15.1–9.17.3","title":"Drug-producing lands, deadly poisons and habituation","summary":"Theophrastus names the regions richest in drugs: Tyrrhenia, Latium and Egypt, as the poets attest with Homer's nepenthes, then Ethiopia, Scythia, India and Thrace, and in Hellas Pelion, Telethrion, Parnassus, Arcadia and Laconia, with their characteristic plants including moly. He describes Cretan dittany, its use in childbirth and the story of goats expelling arrows, and wolf's-bane of Herakleia, a poison said to act after a set interval and without antidote, contrasted with meadow saffron. He reports Thrasyas of Mantineia's painless poison of hemlock and poppy and the improved Cean preparation of hemlock. He ends by arguing that familiarity weakens drugs, citing hellebore-eating shepherds and the two Eudemuses.","ideas":["Poets point to Tyrrhenia and Egypt as lands of drugs","Dittany is unique to Crete and eases labour","Wolf's-bane is said to kill after a delay matching its time since gathering","Habit and constitution make poisons harmless to some"],"terms":[{"t":"nepenthes","d":"Homer's Egyptian drug that brings forgetfulness of sorrow, given to Helen by Polydamna."},{"t":"akoniton (wolf's-bane)","d":"A deadly root named from Akonai among the Mariandynoi, for which no antidote is said to exist."}]},{"i":747,"j":760,"loc":"9.18.1–9.20.8","title":"Strange virtues, charms and heating drugs","summary":"Theophrastus reports plants that act on lifeless things, thickening water or setting meat, and the scorpion-plant that kills a scorpion which white hellebore revives, allowing that such tales are not wholly baseless. He notes plants affecting the mind, such as oleander root that cheers, but calls the claims for amulets, tripolion, and plants granting fair fame foolish boasts of craftsmen. He raises the question whether plants with the same effect share one common virtue or reach it by different causes. He closes with heating and purging drugs, pepper, Cnidian berry, bryony, thapsia, birthwort and male-fern, observes that drugs from wintry, north-facing, dry places are best, and summarises the whole book.","ideas":["Some plants act on inanimate things as the magnet or amber does","Amulets and fame-giving plants are dismissed as self-glorifying tales","Open question: do like effects come from one common virtue or different causes?","The best drugs come from cold, dry, north-facing places"],"terms":[{"t":"dynamis (virtue, power)","d":"The active property of a root, fruit or juice, shown in its effects on bodies, minds or lifeless things."}]}]});